.TH "MSG" "1" "" "msg 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
msg \(em send one text message to stdout, Slack, or several destinations
.SH SYNOPSIS
.IP
.EX
msg [OPTIONS] [\-\-] [MESSAGE ...]
msg [\-\-config PATH] account add NAME [\-\-type slack] [\-\-token\-env NAME | \-\-token\-file PATH]
msg [\-\-config PATH] config path
msg [\-\-config PATH] config list
msg [\-\-config PATH] config get KEY
msg [\-\-config PATH] config set KEY VALUE
msg [\-\-config PATH] config unset KEY
msg [\-\-config PATH] config secret set ACCOUNT.token
msg [\-\-config PATH] config secret delete ACCOUNT.token
msg help [COMMAND ...]
.EE
.SH DESCRIPTION
\f[CR]msg\f[R] reads one UTF\-8 message from arguments or standard
input, delivers it synchronously, and exits with a status suitable for
shell scripts.
Linux and macOS are supported.
With no \f[CR]\-\-to\f[R], it writes to stdout; Slack delivery always
requires an explicit \f[CR]\-\-to slack:RECIPIENT\f[R].
.PP
Configuration stores credential references and application settings,
never recipient locations.
Selecting an account does not select a destination.
There are no background queues, automatic retries, streaming sends,
email destinations, or incoming\-webhook destinations.
.SH QUICK START
Prebuilt Linux and macOS packages are available from \c
.UR https://github.com/sparkgeo/msg/releases
GitHub Releases
.UE \c
\&.
Choose your platform and architecture.
On Debian/Ubuntu, install the matching \f[CR].deb\f[R] with
\f[CR]sudo apt install ./msg_VERSION_ARCH.deb\f[R].
For a \f[CR].tar.gz\f[R] archive, extract it and run
\f[CR]./install.sh\f[R] from the extracted directory to install the
binary and manual under \f[CR]$HOME/.local\f[R]; keep
\f[CR]$HOME/.local/bin\f[R] on \f[CR]PATH\f[R].
An absolute prefix can be supplied, for example
\f[CR]sudo ./install.sh /usr/local\f[R].
Release archives include installation details and example configuration.
.PP
Build and install from a source checkout with Rust 1.88 or newer, a
native compiler toolchain, and Make:
.IP
.EX
make install
msg \(aqHello from the terminal\(aq
printf \(aqHello from stdin\(rsn\(aq \f[B]|\f[R] msg
man msg
.EE
.PP
\f[CR]make install\f[R] installs the binary and the prebuilt manual
under \f[CR]PREFIX\f[R], which defaults to \f[CR]$CARGO_HOME\f[R] when
set, otherwise \f[CR]$HOME/.cargo\f[R].
Keep \f[CR]PREFIX/bin\f[R] on \f[CR]PATH\f[R].
No documentation generator is needed to install.
For another prefix, use
\f[CR]make install PREFIX=\(dq$HOME/.local\(dq\f[R].
.PP
\f[CR]cargo install \-\-path . \-\-locked\f[R] installs only the binary.
Add the manual separately with \f[CR]make install\-man\f[R], using the
same prefix as the binary.
\f[CR]make install\-man MANDIR=/usr/local/share/man\f[R] selects a
system manual directory when you have permission to write there.
.PP
Read the manual without installing with \f[CR]man ./man/msg.1\f[R] from
the checkout.
In the usual \f[CR]man\f[R] pager, \f[CR]/timeout\f[R] searches for
text, \f[CR]n\f[R] finds the next match, and \f[CR]q\f[R] quits.
If \f[CR]man\f[R] is unavailable, install your operating system\(aqs
manual viewer (for example, \f[CR]man\-db\f[R] on Ubuntu).
.PP
For Slack, first create and install a Slack app with the scopes
described in \f[B]Slack setup\f[R], then configure an account and store
its token:
.IP
.EX
msg account add work \-\-type slack
msg config secret set work.token
msg \-\-to \(aqslack:#team\(aq \(aqDeployment complete\(aq
.EE
.PP
Replace example channel names, usernames, and IDs with your own.
The secret command prompts in the terminal with echo disabled.
One configured account is selected automatically for all Slack
destinations.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\f[B]\-\-config PATH\f[R]
Use this TOML file instead of the default configuration path.
Relative paths are resolved from the working directory.
This global option can appear before or after a \f[CR]config\f[R] or
\f[CR]account\f[R] subcommand.
An explicitly selected file must exist when sending.
Configuration commands can inspect defaults or create a file even when
this path does not yet exist.
.TP
\f[B]\-t DESTINATION, \-\-to DESTINATION\f[R]
Select a destination.
Repeat to select several.
Supported forms are \f[CR]stdout\f[R], \f[CR]slack:#channel\f[R],
\f[CR]slack:\(atusername\f[R], and \f[CR]slack:ID\f[R].
Default when omitted: \f[CR]stdout\f[R].
Once any \f[CR]\-\-to\f[R] is present, stdout is included only if
explicitly selected or added with \f[CR]\-\-passthrough\f[R].
See \f[B]Destinations\f[R] for syntax, ordering, and duplicate handling.
.TP
\f[B]\-\-account NAME\f[R]
Select the configured Slack account for every Slack destination in this
invocation.
Overrides \f[CR]default_slack_account\f[R].
The name must exist even if only stdout is selected.
This option never changes the recipient.
.TP
\f[B]\-p, \-\-passthrough\f[R]
Add stdout as the first destination.
Stdout receives the entire original message, even when
\f[CR]\-\-max\-chars\f[R] shortens the Slack message.
If stdout was already selected, it is still written only once.
All preflight checks finish before any output, including passthrough
output.
.TP
\f[B]\-\-no\-newline\f[R]
Suppress the extra newline normally appended to argument messages on
stdout.
Existing newlines are preserved.
Does not change piped input or Slack text.
The default is to append a newline for argument messages.
.TP
\f[B]\-n N, \-\-max\-chars N\f[R]
Send only the first N Unicode scalar values to Slack.
N must be a positive integer; zero and negative values are errors.
Default: no truncation.
Stdout stays complete.
Counting is by scalar value, not UTF\-8 byte or displayed grapheme: an
emoji sequence or a combining character may be split.
No ellipsis is added.
Truncation occurs before Slack escaping; the escaped message must still
fit the 40,000\-character limit.
This option does \f[B]not\f[R] suppress the stdout newline.
.TP
\f[B]\-\-result\-file PATH\f[R]
Save a JSON array of per\-destination results after delivery.
The parent directory must already exist and the path must not exist.
Relative paths are relative to the working directory.
\f[CR]\-\f[R] is rejected; results never replace stdout.
The report contains neither message text nor token values.
See \f[B]Delivery and results\f[R] for the schema and failure behavior.
.TP
\f[B]\-h, \-\-help\f[R]
Print command help and exit successfully.
Available on subcommands too; for example,
\f[CR]msg config set \-\-help\f[R].
\f[CR]\-\-help\f[R] provides the longer help form where Clap has
additional detail.
The generated \f[CR]help\f[R] subcommand also accepts a command path,
such as \f[CR]msg help config secret set\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]\-V, \-\-version\f[R]
Print the application version and exit successfully.
.TP
\f[B]\-\-\f[R]
End option and subcommand parsing.
Use before message text that begins with a hyphen or would otherwise be
recognized as a subcommand, such as \f[CR]msg \-\- config list\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]MESSAGE ...\f[R]
Message words, joined with one space between arguments.
Omit all message arguments to read stdin through EOF.
Quote text to preserve spaces inside an argument.
An empty message is rejected.
.PP
Send options cannot be combined with \f[CR]config\f[R] subcommands.
Only \f[CR]\-\-config\f[R] is global.
Put send options before the message for clarity.
.SH INPUT AND OUTPUT
Argument messages behave like basic \f[CR]echo\f[R]: the shell supplies
the arguments, \f[CR]msg\f[R] joins them with spaces, and stdout appends
a newline.
If the argument already ends with a newline, the added newline is still
appended unless \f[CR]\-\-no\-newline\f[R] is set.
Literal backslashes are not interpreted as escape sequences.
Slack receives the joined message without an added newline.
.IP
.EX
msg Hello world                  \f[I]# stdout: Hello world plus newline\f[R]
msg \(aqHello   world\(aq              \f[I]# preserve the three spaces\f[R]
msg \-\-no\-newline \(aqWorking...\(aq    \f[I]# no added newline\f[R]
msg \(aqfirst\(rsnsecond\(aq              \f[I]# literal backslash and n\f[R]
msg \-\- \(aq\-a message beginning with a flag\(aq
msg \-\- config list              \f[I]# send the words \(dqconfig list\(dq\f[R]
printf \(aqfirst\(rsnsecond\(rsn\(aq \f[B]|\f[R] msg   \f[I]# two lines, preserved exactly\f[R]
.EE
.PP
Piped or redirected stdin is preserved exactly on stdout, including
leading whitespace, CRLF, and trailing newlines.
Input is buffered through EOF before delivery.
Both argument and stdin messages are limited to 8 MiB (8,388,608 UTF\-8
bytes); \f[CR]\-n\f[R] does not increase that limit.
Invalid UTF\-8 and empty input are errors.
Whitespace\-only input is allowed locally, though Slack may reject it.
Running \f[CR]msg\f[R] without arguments on an interactive terminal is
an error; it does not start an interactive message editor.
.PP
An argument plus nonempty stdin is rejected to avoid silently discarding
input.
Empty redirected stdin is allowed with an argument:
.IP
.EX
msg \(aqService started\(aq </dev/null
msg < report.txt
.EE
.PP
An ongoing stream such as \f[CR]tail \-f\f[R] does not produce
incremental deliveries; \f[CR]msg\f[R] waits for EOF (or the input
limit).
Redirecting stdout does not add a stdout destination when Slack was
explicitly selected.
.PP
Slack messages are sent as plain text.
\f[CR]&\f[R], \f[CR]<\f[R], and \f[CR]>\f[R] are escaped; markup,
automatic mention/link parsing, and link/media previews are disabled.
Stdout is never Slack\-escaped.
The local Slack size check is 40,000 Unicode scalar values \f[B]after
escaping\f[R], so text containing many ampersands or angle brackets may
need a smaller \f[CR]\-n\f[R] value.
.SH DESTINATIONS
.TP
\f[B]stdout\f[R]
Write the original message to standard output.
No account or token is needed.
Configuration must still be valid.
.TP
\f[B]slack:#channel\f[R]
Look up an exact Slack channel name, excluding archived channels.
Lookup requests include public and private channels and need the
corresponding read scopes.
Private channels must be accessible to the bot.
.TP
\f[B]slack:\(atusername\f[R]
Look up the exact Slack API \f[CR]name\f[R], excluding deleted users.
Display names and full names are not matched.
The bot opens a DM with that user before posting; this is not a message
in your personal DM conversation.
.TP
\f[B]slack:C\&..., slack:G\&..., slack:D\&...\f[R]
Send to a channel/conversation ID directly, including an existing DM ID.
Bypasses directory lookup.
Permissions and bot access still apply.
.TP
\f[B]slack:U\&..., slack:W\&...\f[R]
Open a bot DM with this user ID and then send the message.
Bypasses username lookup but still requires permission to open the DM.
.PP
Names after \f[CR]#\f[R] or \f[CR]\(at\f[R] must be nonempty, at most
255 UTF\-8 bytes, and contain only Unicode letters/digits, hyphens,
underscores, or periods.
IDs must start with one of the uppercase prefixes above, contain at
least two characters, and consist entirely of uppercase ASCII letters
and digits.
These are local syntax checks; Slack determines whether the recipient
exists.
Destination strings are case\-sensitive.
Unprefixed aliases and other service prefixes are unsupported.
Tokens are never accepted inside a destination.
.PP
Repeated identical destination strings are delivered once, in
first\-occurrence order.
\f[CR]\-\-passthrough\f[R] puts stdout first.
Different strings that resolve to the same recipient, such as a name and
its ID, are separate deliveries.
.PP
All directory lookups finish before any message is sent or stdout
written.
Lookup follows pagination and requires exactly one matching ID.
Unknown or inaccessible names, ambiguous matches, missing scopes,
malformed responses, and lookup rate limits fail preflight with exit
code 2.
Each complete lookup has a \f[CR]timeout_seconds\f[R] budget across its
pages and a 1,000\-page safety limit.
No directory cache is persisted between invocations.
IDs avoid these lookup requests and their additional read scopes.
.SH CONFIGURATION
.SS File selection and defaults
Configuration is TOML.
The file is selected in this order:
.IP "1." 3
\f[CR]\-\-config PATH\f[R], when supplied.
.IP "2." 3
\f[CR]$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/msg/config.toml\f[R] when
\f[CR]XDG_CONFIG_HOME\f[R] is nonempty and absolute.
.IP "3." 3
\f[CR]$HOME/.config/msg/config.toml\f[R] otherwise, including when
\f[CR]XDG_CONFIG_HOME\f[R] is empty or relative.
.PP
Linux and macOS use the same convention.
Nothing is read from or written to \f[CR]/etc\f[R] automatically.
If neither a usable XDG path nor HOME is available, use
\f[CR]\-\-config\f[R].
Paths inside TOML do not expand \f[CR]\(ti\f[R] or \f[CR]$VARIABLE\f[R];
use a literal absolute path for file credentials.
.PP
A missing default file means built\-in defaults.
Sending with an explicit missing file is an error.
An existing invalid file is an error even for stdout\-only sends.
Unknown fields, wrong types, and invalid values are rejected.
There are no environment overrides for the settings below.
.PP
The complete default configuration is:
.IP
.EX
timeout_seconds = 30
\f[B][accounts]\f[R]
.EE
.PP
\f[CR]default_slack_account\f[R] is absent by default.
There are no implicit accounts, secrets, or remote recipients.
.SS timeout_seconds
Integer from \f[B]1 through 300\f[R], inclusive.
Default: \f[B]30\f[R].
Controls the total timeout of each Slack HTTP request.
Connection setup is also bounded by the smaller of this value and 10
seconds.
A paginated name lookup shares one timeout budget across all its pages.
Opening a DM and posting its message are separate requests.
Multiple destinations are sent sequentially, so this is not a deadline
for the whole command.
It does not limit waiting for stdin or replace the keyring\(aqs own
timeouts.
.IP
.EX
msg config set timeout_seconds 60
msg config get timeout_seconds
msg config unset timeout_seconds   \f[I]# return to 30\f[R]
.EE
.SS default_slack_account
Optional string naming the account to use when \f[CR]\-\-account\f[R] is
absent.
There is no default value.
Names use ASCII letters, digits, underscores, and hyphens and must not
be empty.
The selected account must exist when used for Slack delivery; a
syntactically valid reference can be saved before creating the account.
.PP
Credential selection is: \f[CR]\-\-account NAME\f[R], then
\f[CR]default_slack_account\f[R], then the sole configured account.
If no account exists, or several exist without a selection, Slack
delivery fails before sending.
A bad explicit/default selection does not fall back to another account.
.IP
.EX
msg config set default_slack_account work
msg \-\-account personal \-\-to \(aqslack:#general\(aq \(aqHello\(aq
msg config unset default_slack_account
.EE
.SS accounts and accounts.NAME.type
\f[CR]accounts\f[R] is a table of named accounts, empty by default.
Account names follow the same rules as \f[CR]default_slack_account\f[R].
Each account requires \f[CR]type\f[R] and \f[CR]token_secret\f[R].
The only supported \f[CR]type\f[R] is the string
\f[CR]\(dqslack\(dq\f[R].
An account represents a token for one Slack app installation/workspace;
the same account can send to many recipients in that workspace.
.IP
.EX
\f[B][accounts.work]\f[R]
type = \(dqslack\(dq

\f[B][accounts.work.token_secret]\f[R]
source = \(dqkeyring\(dq
name = \(dqmsg/work/token\(dq
.EE
.SS accounts.NAME.token_secret
Required table describing \f[B]where\f[R] to retrieve the token, never
the token itself.
Exactly one of these three shapes is allowed:
.IP
.EX
\f[I]# Desktop credential store:\f[R]
token_secret = { source = \(dqkeyring\(dq, name = \(dqmsg/work/token\(dq }

\f[I]# A secret supplied by a CI runner or parent process:\f[R]
token_secret = { source = \(dqenv\(dq, name = \(dqMSG_SLACK_TOKEN\(dq }

\f[I]# A protected file for a service or scheduled job:\f[R]
token_secret = { source = \(dqfile\(dq, path = \(dq/absolute/path/slack\-token\(dq }
.EE
.PP
These are alternatives: put only one \f[CR]token_secret\f[R] in each
account.
.TP
\f[B]source\f[R]
Required string, exactly \f[CR]\(dqkeyring\(dq\f[R],
\f[CR]\(dqenv\(dq\f[R], or \f[CR]\(dqfile\(dq\f[R].
There is no default and no fallback if the selected source is
unavailable.
.TP
\f[B]name\f[R]
Required for \f[CR]keyring\f[R] and \f[CR]env\f[R]; not allowed for
\f[CR]file\f[R].
Must be nonempty and contain no control characters.
For \f[CR]keyring\f[R], this is the entry name.
For \f[CR]env\f[R], it is the exact, case\-sensitive environment
variable name, not its value; ordinary shell\-compatible names such as
\f[CR]MSG_SLACK_TOKEN\f[R] are easiest to provision.
.TP
\f[B]path\f[R]
Required for \f[CR]file\f[R]; not allowed for \f[CR]keyring\f[R] or
\f[CR]env\f[R].
Must be an absolute path to a protected regular file.
The file is read when Slack credentials are needed, not by
\f[CR]config list\f[R] or \f[CR]config get\f[R].
.PP
Changing source usually requires replacing the whole
\f[CR]token_secret\f[R] table so old fields do not conflict with the new
shape:
.IP
.EX
msg config set accounts.work.token_secret \(rs
  \(aq{ source = \(dqenv\(dq, name = \(dqMSG_SLACK_TOKEN\(dq }\(aq
.EE
.SS Complete multi\-account example
.IP
.EX
timeout_seconds = 45
default_slack_account = \(dqwork\(dq

\f[B][accounts.work]\f[R]
type = \(dqslack\(dq
token_secret = { source = \(dqkeyring\(dq, name = \(dqmsg/work/token\(dq }

\f[B][accounts.personal]\f[R]
type = \(dqslack\(dq
token_secret = { source = \(dqenv\(dq, name = \(dqPERSONAL_SLACK_TOKEN\(dq }

\f[B][accounts.automation]\f[R]
type = \(dqslack\(dq
token_secret = { source = \(dqfile\(dq, path = \(dq/run/credentials/notify/slack\-token\(dq }
.EE
.PP
The example credential file must be provisioned before using
\f[CR]automation\f[R].
Setting a default account still leaves \f[CR]msg \(aqHello\(aq\f[R] as
stdout\-only.
To send with credentials from different accounts, use separate
invocations:
.IP
.EX
msg \-\-account work \-\-to \(aqslack:#team\(aq \(aqWork update\(aq
msg \-\-account personal \-\-to \(aqslack:#general\(aq \(aqPersonal update\(aq
.EE
.SH CONFIGURATION COMMANDS
.TP
\f[B]msg account add NAME [\-\-type slack] [\-\-token\-env NAME | \-\-token\-file PATH]\f[R]
Create a complete account in one write.
The type defaults to \f[CR]slack\f[R], currently the only supported
service.
Without a credential option, create a keyring reference named
\f[CR]msg/NAME/token\f[R]; store the token afterward with
\f[CR]msg config secret set NAME.token\f[R].
\f[CR]\-\-token\-env\f[R] references an environment variable, and
\f[CR]\-\-token\-file\f[R] references an absolute file path.
These options are mutually exclusive.
Credentials are provisioned separately and are not read or checked by
this command.
Existing accounts are never overwritten.
A missing configuration file and its parent directories are created.
The command does not set a default account; a sole account is selected
automatically, and multiple accounts need a default or
\f[CR]\-\-account\f[R].
.PP
Examples:
.IP
.EX
msg account add work
msg config secret set work.token
msg account add ci \-\-token\-env MSG_SLACK_TOKEN
msg account add job \-\-token\-file /absolute/path/to/slack\-token
msg config set default_slack_account work
.EE
.TP
\f[B]msg config path\f[R]
Print the absolute selected configuration path.
Does not read, validate, or create the file.
.TP
\f[B]msg config list\f[R]
Print the effective configuration as TOML, including defaults and secret
references.
Does not resolve tokens or prove the credentials work.
A missing file prints defaults without creating it.
.TP
\f[B]msg config get KEY\f[R]
Print a value from the effective configuration using a dotted key, such
as \f[CR]accounts.work.type\f[R].
Strings are printed without surrounding TOML quotes; other values use
TOML representation.
Missing keys are errors.
For example, an unset \f[CR]timeout_seconds\f[R] reads as \f[CR]30\f[R],
while an unset \f[CR]default_slack_account\f[R] has no value and is an
error.
.TP
\f[B]msg config set KEY VALUE\f[R]
Set a dotted key using the setting\(aqs type.
String settings take literal text, including values such as
\f[CR]123\f[R], \f[CR]true\f[R], or \f[CR][entry]\f[R].
Use shell quotes for spaces, but do not embed TOML quotes; any quotes
passed through the shell become part of the stored string.
\f[CR]timeout_seconds\f[R] takes an integer from 1 through 300.
Whole tables still accept TOML syntax, including quoted strings inside
those tables, for advanced edits.
Each dotted key component must follow the account\-name character rules.
The entire resulting configuration is validated before writing.
Use \f[CR]account add\f[R] to create accounts, because partial accounts
are invalid.
Missing parent directories and a missing configuration file are created.
.PP
Existing TOML files need no migration.
Older scripts that embedded TOML quotes around individual string values
should remove them: use
\f[CR]msg config set default_slack_account work\f[R] instead of
\f[CR]msg config set default_slack_account \(aq\(dqwork\(dq\(aq\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]msg config unset KEY\f[R]
Remove a stored setting.
Built\-in defaults apply again where defined.
Unlike \f[CR]get\f[R], this operates on the file, so removing a key that
was never stored is an error.
The remaining configuration must be valid; remove a whole account
instead of removing one of its required fields.
Removing an account does not delete its keyring entry, file, or
environment variable.
.TP
\f[B]msg config secret set ACCOUNT.token\f[R]
Create or replace the keyring entry referenced by this configured
account.
Requires a terminal for a hidden token prompt.
The account must already exist and use
\f[CR]source = \(dqkeyring\(dq\f[R].
OS authorization/unlock prompts may occur during setup.
A \f[CR].token\f[R] suffix is required; this is an account reference,
not the keyring entry\(aqs name.
No token value is accepted as an argument or via piped stdin.
.TP
\f[B]msg config secret delete ACCOUNT.token\f[R]
Delete that account\(aqs configured keyring entry.
Keeps the account and secret reference in TOML; future Slack sends fail
until the entry is restored or the reference changes.
File and environment secrets must be managed outside \f[CR]msg\f[R].
Accounts sharing one keyring name share the entry, so replacing or
deleting it affects all of them.
.IP
.EX
msg config path
msg config list
msg config get accounts.work.token_secret.source
msg config set accounts.work.token_secret.name msg/work/new\-token
msg config secret set work.token

\f[I]# Use an isolated configuration; account add creates it if needed.\f[R]
msg \-\-config ./job.toml account add job \-\-token\-env JOB_TOKEN
msg config list \-\-config ./job.toml

\f[I]# Delete a keyring entry before removing its account reference.\f[R]
msg config secret delete work.token
msg config unset accounts.work
\f[I]# Remove a default referring to work too, if it was stored:\f[R]
msg config unset default_slack_account
.EE
.PP
Writes replace the configuration atomically with private file
permissions and preserve comments where possible.
Newly created directories use mode 0700; existing directory permissions
are not changed.
Concurrent writers are not coordinated: serialize configuration updates
to avoid lost changes.
Rejected edits leave the previous file intact.
Source lines are omitted from parse errors to avoid exposing
accidentally pasted secrets.
.SS Migrating older configurations
\f[CR]default_outputs\f[R], \f[CR]default_destination\f[R], and
\f[CR][destinations]\f[R] are obsolete and rejected.
Named destination aliases are also unsupported.
Remove only the entries present in your file, then pass recipients
through \f[CR]\-\-to\f[R]:
.IP
.EX
msg config unset default_outputs
msg config unset destinations
msg config unset default_destination
msg \-\-to \(aqslack:#team\(aq \(aqUsing an explicit recipient\(aq
.EE
.PP
These removals can be performed one at a time while other obsolete
recipient settings remain.
Accounts and unrelated comments are preserved.
For a different file, add \f[CR]\-\-config PATH\f[R].
No automatic migration sends to old recipients or rewrites your
configuration.
.SH CREDENTIALS
All resolved tokens must be nonempty, at most 16 KiB (16,384 bytes), and
contain only printable ASCII without spaces.
Environment and keyring tokens are not whitespace\-trimmed.
File contents must fit the 16 KiB limit before trailing CR/LF characters
are stripped.
Embedded newlines and other whitespace are invalid.
Tokens are resolved only for accounts needed by selected Slack
destinations.
.SS Keyring
Linux uses Secret Service on the session D\-Bus, with attributes
\f[CR]application=msg\f[R] and \f[CR]name=NAME\f[R].
Create a default collection in your desktop credential manager before
storing a token.
Linux D\-Bus method calls have a five\-second timeout.
macOS uses Keychain service \f[CR]msg\f[R] with account \f[CR]NAME\f[R].
Sending never requests an unlock/authorization prompt: missing, locked,
inaccessible, or ambiguous entries fail instead.
Unlock/authorize the store interactively before sending, or choose a
file/env source for unattended jobs.
.IP
.EX
msg config secret set work.token       \f[I]# store or rotate\f[R]
msg config secret delete work.token    \f[I]# revoke local stored copy\f[R]
.EE
.PP
Deleting a local copy does not revoke the token at Slack.
.SS File
The credential path must be a regular file, readable by the process,
owned by the current effective user or root, and have no group/other
permission bits.
Typical modes are 0600 or 0400.
A symlink at the credential path is rejected; symlinks in parent
directories are not checked by this rule.
Keep containing directories private, typically 0700.
The token is stored as access\-controlled plaintext, not encrypted by
\f[CR]msg\f[R].
.PP
In Bash, provision a new private file without putting the token on a
command line or in shell history:
.IP
.EX
mkdir \-p \(dq$HOME/.config/msg/secrets\(dq
chmod 700 \(dq$HOME/.config/msg/secrets\(dq
\f[B](\f[R]
  umask 077
  read \-r \-s \-p \(aqSlack token: \(aq token
  printf \(aq\(rsn\(aq >&2
  printf \(aq%s\(rsn\(aq \(dq$token\(dq > \(dq$HOME/.config/msg/secrets/slack\-token\(dq
\f[B])\f[R]
chmod 600 \(dq$HOME/.config/msg/secrets/slack\-token\(dq
.EE
.PP
Then set the reference, replacing \f[CR]/home/alice\f[R] with your
actual home path:
.IP
.EX
msg config set accounts.work.token_secret \(rs
  \(aq{ source = \(dqfile\(dq, path = \(dq/home/alice/.config/msg/secrets/slack\-token\(dq }\(aq
.EE
.PP
For mounted secrets, reference a protected regular file.
A symlink supplied by an orchestrator is not accepted directly.
Provision and remove these files outside \f[CR]msg\f[R].
.SS Environment
The named variable must be exported into \f[CR]msg\f[R]\(aqs
environment.
A CI secret can be mapped to \f[CR]MSG_SLACK_TOKEN\f[R], with this
account configuration:
.IP
.EX
\f[B][accounts.ci]\f[R]
type = \(dqslack\(dq
token_secret = { source = \(dqenv\(dq, name = \(dqMSG_SLACK_TOKEN\(dq }
.EE
.PP
For a temporary interactive Bash session:
.IP
.EX
read \-r \-s \-p \(aqSlack token: \(aq MSG_SLACK_TOKEN
printf \(aq\(rsn\(aq >&2
export MSG_SLACK_TOKEN
msg \-\-account ci \-\-to slack:C0123456789 \(aqCI completed\(aq
unset MSG_SLACK_TOKEN
.EE
.PP
Environment secrets can be exposed by debug output or inherited by child
processes.
Avoid shell tracing while provisioning credentials.
\f[CR]config secret\f[R] commands do not set or unset environment
variables.
.PP
Ordinary configuration output, result files, and request diagnostics
omit resolved token values.
Owned token buffers are zeroed when dropped where controlled by
\f[CR]msg\f[R]; HTTP and OS libraries may make internal copies.
Do not put tokens in message text, destination strings, or ordinary TOML
fields.
.SH SLACK SETUP
Create a Slack app, grant the appropriate \f[B]bot token\f[R] scopes,
and install it into the workspace.
Store the installation\(aqs token in your chosen secret source.
Invite the bot to the channels where it will post.
.TP
\f[B]chat:write\f[R]
Send messages.
Required for all Slack posting.
.TP
\f[B]chat:write.public\f[R]
Post to public channels without joining, in addition to
\f[CR]chat:write\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]channels:read\f[R]
Look up public channel names.
.TP
\f[B]groups:read\f[R]
Look up private channel names.
.TP
\f[B]users:read\f[R]
Look up usernames.
.TP
\f[B]im:write\f[R]
Open a DM from a username or user ID.
.PP
Private channels require bot membership.
A bot does not inherit your personal access to private conversations and
cannot generally post into an existing DM between other people.
Supplying a channel or existing DM ID avoids directory lookup; supplying
a user ID still requires opening a DM.
If you change scopes, reinstall/re\-authorize the app to update its
grants.
.PP
The production API endpoint is fixed to
\f[CR]https://slack.com/api\f[R].
HTTP redirects and automatic retries are disabled.
HTTP 200 alone does not mean success: \f[CR]msg\f[R] checks Slack\(aqs
\f[CR]ok\f[R] field.
The HTTP client uses standard proxy environment settings; there is no
msg\-specific proxy or endpoint configuration key.
.PP
API details: \c
.UR https://docs.slack.dev/reference/methods/chat.postMessage/
chat.postMessage
.UE \c
, \c
.UR https://docs.slack.dev/reference/methods/conversations.open/
conversations.open
.UE \c
, \c
.UR https://docs.slack.dev/reference/methods/conversations.list/
conversations.list
.UE \c
, and \c
.UR https://docs.slack.dev/reference/methods/users.list/
users.list
.UE \c
\&.
.SH DELIVERY AND RESULTS
Before any delivery, \f[CR]msg\f[R] validates the configuration, input,
destination syntax, selected credentials, escaped Slack length,
result\-file destination, and directory lookups.
A preflight failure prints diagnostics to stderr, returns 2, writes no
stdout message, sends no message, and creates no result file.
Preflight may read credentials and make directory lookup requests; it is
not an offline dry run.
.PP
After preflight, delivery is sequential in destination order.
Every destination is attempted even if another fails, including when
stdout has a broken pipe.
Opening a user DM happens during this delivery phase; a DM open failure
is a destination failure and other deliveries still proceed.
There is no transaction or rollback across destinations.
.PP
Success means stdout was written/flushed, or Slack accepted the API
request.
It does not mean a human read the message.
Timeouts or interrupted/invalid responses can leave acceptance
uncertain.
No automatic retries occur.
Rerunning a command can duplicate messages already accepted.
.PP
Successful sends are silent except for selected stdout output.
Diagnostics go to stderr.
With \f[CR]\-\-result\-file\f[R], an example successful report is:
.IP
.EX
[
  { \(dqdestination\(dq: \(dqstdout\(dq, \(dqstatus\(dq: \(dqwritten\(dq },
  { \(dqdestination\(dq: \(dqslack:C0123456789\(dq, \(dqstatus\(dq: \(dqaccepted\(dq }
]
.EE
.PP
Each array entry has the original destination string and one status:
.TP
\f[B]written\f[R]
Stdout was written and flushed successfully.
.TP
\f[B]accepted\f[R]
Slack acknowledged the message.
.TP
\f[B]failed\f[R]
The destination failed; an \f[CR]error\f[R] string explains why.
Stdout failure may leave partial output.
A rejected Slack request, rate limit, connection failure, or failure to
open a DM is reported here.
.TP
\f[B]uncertain\f[R]
Slack acceptance could not be determined; an \f[CR]error\f[R] string
explains why.
Examples include request interruption, server errors, or malformed
posting responses.
Check the destination before deciding to resend.
.PP
The report is written atomically with private permissions and refuses to
overwrite an existing file, including a file created at the same path
during delivery.
The parent directory must already exist.
If saving fails after deliveries were attempted, exit code 5 overrides
the delivery outcome.
There is no report for a preflight failure.
Reports omit message bodies and credentials but do include recipient
labels.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
\f[B]0\f[R]
All destinations succeeded, or a configuration/help/version command
succeeded.
.TP
\f[B]2\f[R]
Usage, input, configuration, credential, or preflight error; no
deliveries started.
.TP
\f[B]3\f[R]
No destination succeeded; acceptance may be uncertain.
.TP
\f[B]4\f[R]
Some destinations succeeded and some failed or are uncertain.
.TP
\f[B]5\f[R]
Deliveries were attempted, but the requested result file could not be
saved.
.SH EXAMPLES
.SS Several destinations and full stdout output
.IP
.EX
msg \-t \(aqslack:#team\(aq \-t \(aqslack:#operations\(aq \(aqDeployment complete\(aq
msg \-p \-t \(aqslack:#team\(aq \-t \(aqslack:\(atalice\(aq \(aqPlease review\(aq
msg \-t stdout \-t slack:C0123456789 \(aqSaved locally and sent remotely\(aq
.EE
.SS Short Slack notification, complete local report
.IP
.EX
\f[I]# report.txt already contains the full report.\f[R]
msg \-\-to \(aqslack:#team\(aq \-\-max\-chars 500 \-\-passthrough \(rs
  < report.txt > report\-copy.txt
.EE
.PP
Only Slack is truncated.
The copied file contains all the original bytes.
.SS Check a pipeline\(aqs status
In Bash, \f[CR]pipefail\f[R] makes failures earlier in a pipeline affect
its exit status.
Without it, the shell usually reports only the last command\(aqs status.
\f[CR]msg\f[R] cannot know whether the upstream producer succeeded; it
may send the partial output of a failed command.
.IP
.EX
set \-o pipefail
printf \(aqBuild complete\(rsn\(aq \f[B]|\f[R] msg \-\-to \(aqslack:#team\(aq \-\-passthrough \f[B]|\f[R] tee notice.txt
.EE
.PP
To send only if a producer succeeds, capture its output first.
Replace \f[CR]./build\-report\f[R] with your actual command:
.IP
.EX
report_dir=$(mktemp \-d)
trap \(aqrm \-rf \(dq$report_dir\(dq\(aq EXIT
\f[B]if\f[R] ./build\-report > \(dq$report_dir/report.txt\(dq\f[B];\f[R] \f[B]then\f[R]
  msg \-\-to \(aqslack:#team\(aq < \(dq$report_dir/report.txt\(dq
\f[B]else\f[R]
  printf \(aq%s\(rsn\(aq \(aqReport failed; nothing sent\(aq >&2
\f[B]fi\f[R]
.EE
.SS Capture results and distinguish failure types
Use a new path inside an existing private directory.
Do not pass a file created by \f[CR]mktemp\f[R] itself:
\f[CR]\-\-result\-file\f[R] refuses existing files.
.IP
.EX
result_dir=$(mktemp \-d)
status=0
msg \-\-to stdout \-\-to \(aqslack:#team\(aq \(rs
  \-\-result\-file \(dq$result_dir/results.json\(dq \(aqDeployment complete\(aq \f[B]||\f[R] status=$?
\f[B]case\f[R] \(dq$status\(dq \f[B]in\f[R]
  0\f[B])\f[R] printf \(aq%s\(rsn\(aq \(aqAll deliveries succeeded\(aq \f[B];;\f[R]
  2\f[B])\f[R] printf \(aq%s\(rsn\(aq \(aqPreflight failed; no messages sent\(aq >&2 \f[B];;\f[R]
  3\f[B]|\f[R]4\f[B])\f[R] printf \(aq%s\(rsn\(aq \(aqInspect results before retrying\(aq >&2 \f[B];;\f[R]
  5\f[B])\f[R] printf \(aq%s\(rsn\(aq \(aqReport missing; deliveries were attempted\(aq >&2 \f[B];;\f[R]
\f[B]esac\f[R]
\f[B]if\f[R] [ \-f \(dq$result_dir/results.json\(dq ]\f[B];\f[R] \f[B]then\f[R]
  cat \(dq$result_dir/results.json\(dq
\f[B]fi\f[R]
.EE
.PP
Keep or remove the result directory after reviewing it.
With \f[CR]jq\f[R] installed,
\f[CR]jq \(aq.[] | select(.status == \(dquncertain\(dq)\(aq PATH\f[R]
selects uncertain results.
.SS Cron and service accounts
Use absolute binary/configuration paths, an explicit \f[CR]\-\-to\f[R],
and a file or environment secret provisioned for the service user.
A desktop keyring may be unavailable outside a login session.
For example, a crontab entry with paths adapted to your installation:
.IP
.EX
0 9 * * * /home/alice/.cargo/bin/msg \-\-config /home/alice/.config/msg/job.toml \-\-to slack:C0123456789 \(aqDaily job started\(aq
.EE
.PP
For systemd, \f[CR]LoadCredential=\f[R] or
\f[CR]LoadCredentialEncrypted=\f[R] can provision a credential file.
Set the account\(aqs \f[CR]token_secret.path\f[R] to its absolute
runtime path.
TOML does not expand systemd specifiers or environment variables.
The supplied file must satisfy \f[B]Credentials \(em File\f[R].
.SS Local\-only checks without Slack
.IP
.EX
msg \-\-version
msg \-\-help
msg config set \-\-help
msg config path
msg config list
printf \(aqLocal test\(rsn\(aq \f[B]|\f[R] msg \-\-to stdout
.EE
.PP
These commands do not send Slack messages.
Configuration inspection does not resolve secrets or verify remote
access.
There is no \f[CR]\-\-dry\-run\f[R] flag.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
\f[B]XDG_CONFIG_HOME\f[R]
Absolute base directory for the default config path.
Empty/relative values fall back to HOME.
It does not choose the manual installation directory.
.TP
\f[B]HOME\f[R]
Used to derive \f[CR]\(ti/.config/msg/config.toml\f[R] when
XDG_CONFIG_HOME is unusable.
Not required for path selection when an explicit config or usable XDG
base is supplied.
.TP
\f[B]Variables named by token_secret.name when source is env\f[R]
Provide account credentials.
There is no fixed built\-in token variable; \f[CR]MSG_SLACK_TOKEN\f[R]
is an example name only.
.TP
\f[B]HTTPS_PROXY, HTTP_PROXY, ALL_PROXY, NO_PROXY\f[R]
Standard proxy environment variables recognized by the underlying HTTP
client (lowercase forms are also supported).
The Slack endpoint is HTTPS, so an HTTPS/all\-protocol proxy is
relevant.
These are not TOML settings.
.TP
\f[B]MANPATH, MANPAGER, PAGER\f[R]
Used by the system \f[CR]man\f[R] viewer, not by \f[CR]msg\f[R].
If the installed manual is not discovered, add its parent man directory
to MANPATH with a trailing colon to retain the viewer\(aqs default
search path:
.IP
.EX
export MANPATH=\(dq$HOME/.cargo/share/man:${MANPATH:\-}\(dq
man msg
.EE
.PP
Adjust the path for your installation prefix.
Add it to your shell startup file if needed.
\f[CR]man \-M \(dq$HOME/.cargo/share/man\(dq msg\f[R] selects the
directory for one invocation.
.SH FILES
.TP
\f[B]$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/msg/config.toml or $HOME/.config/msg/config.toml\f[R]
Default settings and credential references.
.TP
\f[B]Files referenced by accounts.NAME.token_secret.path\f[R]
Plaintext token files with restricted ownership and permissions.
.TP
\f[B]PATH supplied to \-\-result\-file\f[R]
Optional private JSON delivery report.
Must be a new file.
.TP
\f[B]PREFIX/share/man/man1/msg.1\f[R]
Installed section 1 manual, unless \f[CR]MANDIR\f[R] was overridden at
installation.
.SH TROUBLESHOOTING
.TP
\f[B]No manual entry for msg\f[R]
Run \f[CR]make install\-man\f[R] from the checkout.
Ensure the corresponding \f[CR]PREFIX/bin\f[R] is on PATH so the viewer
can infer \f[CR]PREFIX/share/man\f[R], or set MANPATH as above.
With a custom prefix, pass the same \f[CR]PREFIX\f[R] or
\f[CR]MANDIR\f[R] when installing the manual.
\f[CR]man ./man/msg.1\f[R] always allows local viewing.
.TP
\f[B]Configuration or schema error\f[R]
Check \f[CR]msg config path\f[R], the TOML syntax, and the exact
keys/types in \f[B]Configuration\f[R].
Inline token values and unknown keys are rejected.
Source text is intentionally omitted from diagnostics.
Remove obsolete recipient settings with \f[CR]config unset\f[R] if
present.
.TP
\f[B]Multiple accounts or unknown account\f[R]
Supply \f[CR]\-\-account NAME\f[R] or set
\f[CR]default_slack_account\f[R] to an existing account.
\f[CR]msg config list\f[R] shows the configured names.
.TP
\f[B]Keyring unavailable, locked, or missing\f[R]
Unlock/authorize the credential store interactively, check the
configured entry name, and store the token using
\f[CR]config secret set ACCOUNT.token\f[R].
On Linux, ensure a session bus and default collection exist.
For services, provision a file/env source instead.
.TP
\f[B]Credential file rejected\f[R]
Use an absolute path to a readable regular file with mode 0600 or 0400,
owned by the process user or root.
Do not point directly at a symlink.
Check token size and whitespace without printing the token to logs.
.TP
\f[B]Slack name not found, ambiguous, or lookup timed out\f[R]
Check the exact channel/API username, workspace, read scopes, and bot
membership.
Try a Slack ID to bypass directory lookup.
Large directories may require a larger timeout; the maximum is 300
seconds.
.TP
\f[B]Slack authentication or scope error\f[R]
Check the selected account and rotate/re\-provision a revoked token.
Grant the required scopes and reinstall/re\-authorize the app.
Config inspection alone does not test authentication.
.TP
\f[B]Slack destination unavailable or DM cannot open\f[R]
Check recipient IDs, bot membership, and \f[CR]im:write\f[R] for user
destinations.
Private conversations are limited by the bot\(aqs access.
.TP
\f[B]Rate limit or uncertain acceptance\f[R]
\f[CR]msg\f[R] does not retry.
A rate\-limit diagnostic may include a retry delay.
Lookup rate limits stop preflight; posting failures appear in delivery
results.
Inspect successful/uncertain destinations before resending.
.TP
\f[B]Message too long\f[R]
Use \f[CR]\-n N\f[R] with a smaller positive value for Slack.
Escaping can increase length.
The 8 MiB input limit applies before truncation; supply smaller input if
that limit is exceeded.
.TP
\f[B]No stdout while sending to Slack\f[R]
Add \f[CR]\-\-passthrough\f[R] or \f[CR]\-\-to stdout\f[R].
Redirection alone does not select it.
Preflight failures suppress stdout even when passthrough was requested.
.TP
\f[B]Command waits indefinitely for piped input\f[R]
Close the producer or use a finite input file.
Input is buffered to EOF; \f[CR]timeout_seconds\f[R] only bounds Slack
requests, not stdin reading.
.TP
\f[B]Result file exists or cannot be saved\f[R]
Choose a new filename in an existing writable directory.
Preflight failures return 2.
Exit 5 means deliveries were attempted before saving failed, so check
recipients before retrying.
.SH SEE ALSO
\f[CR]man(1)\f[R], \f[CR]printf(1)\f[R], \f[CR]chmod(1)\f[R],
\f[CR]crontab(5)\f[R], \f[CR]systemd.exec(5)\f[R].
.PP
Use \f[CR]msg \-\-help\f[R], \f[CR]msg account add \-\-help\f[R],
\f[CR]msg config \-\-help\f[R], and
\f[CR]msg config secret \-\-help\f[R] for compact command summaries.
The web and terminal manuals are generated from the same
\f[CR]docs/manual.md\f[R] source in the project repository.
